   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to payments for home and\ncommunity based long term care services",
    "basePrintNo": "S8473",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8473-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 60,
       "fullName": "Chris Jacobs",
       "imgName": "921_Chris_Jacobs.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1099,
       "sessionMemberId": 1095,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "JACOBS"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §3614-f, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8473\n\nSPONSOR: HANNON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to payments for home\nand community based long term care services\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend the Public Health Law, in relation to prohibiting claims\nsubmitted by home care providers from being subject to managed care risk\nadjustments on insurers, and requiring any funds appropriated by the\nState to compensate for labor mandates to be fully and timely disbursed\nto home care providers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends the Public Health Law by adding a new\nsection 3614-f to prohibit claims submitted under the medical assistance\nprogram for home and community based long term care services from being\nsubject to managed care risk adjustments. It requires any funds appro-\npriated to Medicaid managed care plans or Medicaid managed long term\ncare plans to compensate for any law or regulation impacting wages to be\nfully distributed to home and community based long term care service\nproviders in amendments to existing contracts ninety days prior to the\neffective date of such labor law or regulation. Additionally, it prohib-\nits such funds appropriated to Medicaid managed care plans or Medicaid\nmanaged long term care plans to compensate for any law or regulation\nimpacting wages from being used to supplant payments for existing\nservices under the Medicaid assistance program.\n\nSection two of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe 2018-19 enacted budget included $703 million for State Fiscal Year\n(SFY) 2018-19 to support the direct care cost of minimum wage increases\nfor Medicaid funded services provided by home health care agencies and\nother providers. However, there is a lack of clarifying language to\nensure that these monies, together with other public funds to address\nstatutory Federal and State requirements, are fully allocated to provid-\ners in a timely manner that allows them to pay workers for such require-\nments.\n\nWithout contract amendments and monies disbursed to home care providers\nfrom plans by the implementation date, providers are still expected to\nmeet the statutory wage obligation and pay home care workers- irrespec-\ntive of plans distributing funds as they are instructed to do by the\nState Department of Health. This bill would create a process by which\nplans must disburse funds to providers 90 days prior to the effective\ndate in order to avoid late contracts and rates, and guarantee that\ninsurers provide the funds in an amount that supplements any current\ncontracts. This bill would also disallow risk adjustment on managed care\nproviders to ensure that the full dollar amounts given by the State to\nthe plans are disbursed in their entirety to home care providers as it\nrelates to all statutory wage obligations. Risk adjustment of funds\ndesignated under the State Budget to address direct care cost increases\n\nfor the minimum wage and other labor costs is inconsistent with the\npurpose of such funding.\n\nEnsuring that all monies appropriated by the State to support the direct\ncare cost of minimum wage increases for home care workers are distrib-\nuted in a timely manner and in their entirety allows home care workers\nto receive their deserved increase in wages, while also protecting the\neconomic viability of the home care industry.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
